1
00:00:24,567 --> 00:00:30,033
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOC's course on Foundations for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:33,496 --> 00:00:39,943
We are discussing a topic related to the optimization of experience.

3
00:00:40,904 --> 00:00:45,529
In particular, we are talking about the optimization of VR navigation experience.

4
00:00:45,909 --> 00:00:52,476
So, we will continue our discussion on that topic in this part of the lecture as well.

5
00:00:52,496 --> 00:00:54,118
Earlier, we talked about

6
00:00:54,790 --> 00:01:11,107
one model for a realistic walking experience; that is, we are assuming that we are having a system-controlled VR tour where user movements and navigation will be controlled by the system.

7
00:01:11,768 --> 00:01:24,561
Now, to provide the user a natural walking experience, we have seen a model for that; we can use that model to provide the user the natural walking experience.

8
00:01:24,963 --> 00:01:40,822
Now, that model is supposed to display a visual speed, or the optic flow, which is the rate at which the scenes change in the virtual world, so that the user gets the perception of movement.

9
00:01:43,325 --> 00:01:52,236
Now, that visual speed is supposed to be provided by the walking model, which matches the user's instantaneous walking speed.

10
00:01:53,330 --> 00:01:57,257
Rather, we should say the perceived instantaneous walking speed.

11
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:00,462
So that is the model.

12
00:02:00,943 --> 00:02:05,631
Now, that model itself does not provide anything related to experience optimization.

13
00:02:06,212 --> 00:02:12,223
It can only be used to enhance presence in the virtual world.

14
00:02:12,884 --> 00:02:18,173
Now, in this lecture, what we are going to do is basically see the use of the model,

15
00:02:19,030 --> 00:02:26,885
and with that model, how we can go for an optimization approach where we will try to optimize for cyber sickness.

16
00:02:29,269 --> 00:02:33,497
Also, it may be noted that in this lecture, we are going to talk about one such approach.

17
00:02:33,558 --> 00:02:34,860
There are many other approaches.

18
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:40,110
In a subsequent part of the lecture, we will talk about another approach as well.

19
00:02:44,883 --> 00:02:57,816
Now, this approach we can broadly term as addressing the cyber sickness concern, that is, optimizing cyber sickness by controlling the exposure duration.

20
00:02:58,297 --> 00:03:05,484
So, earlier we talked about one of the factors of cyber sickness being the duration of exposure in the virtual world.

21
00:03:05,504 --> 00:03:14,113
So here, our objective would be to try to optimize the exposure duration so that cyber sickness can be optimized.

22
00:03:15,477 --> 00:03:22,988
Details about this can be found at the reference mentioned at the end of the overall lecture, reference number 4.

23
00:03:23,669 --> 00:03:30,739
We will point out the relevant reference when we reach the end of the lecture.

24
00:03:33,924 --> 00:03:36,287
Now, if you can recollect,

25
00:03:38,123 --> 00:03:46,814
cyber sickness is correlated to the exposure duration in a VR environment, as we have repeatedly seen in our discussions on cyber sickness.

26
00:03:47,415 --> 00:03:52,662
Now, the degree of the symptoms rises proportionally with the VR session duration.

27
00:03:52,702 --> 00:03:59,190
The more the duration is, the more the exposure is, the more severe the symptoms will be.

28
00:04:02,554 --> 00:04:04,857
So, clearly, shorter duration exposure,

29
00:04:05,545 --> 00:04:17,901
if possible, can help in getting rid of the cyber sickness problem or at least can help in minimizing the onset of cyber sickness.

30
00:04:19,623 --> 00:04:29,075
So, that is the basic assumption on which our approach will be built.

31
00:04:30,377 --> 00:04:34,422
Now, the duration of exposure to cyber sickness

32
00:04:34,672 --> 00:04:43,056
depends on several factors, such as the size of the virtual world, the travelling speed, or the travelling time,

33
00:04:43,056 --> 00:04:55,813
interaction time, such as interaction with objects, such as inspection, selection, and manipulation of objects.

34
00:04:55,833 --> 00:04:56,974
So, there are several factors.

35
00:04:59,376 --> 00:05:02,299
The size of the virtual world, let us see how it affects.

36
00:05:03,005 --> 00:05:17,763
Now, a VR environment can be very small, such as a room, or very large, like a city, or sometimes even larger, such as the Majuli Island that we talked about in our case study.

37
00:05:20,506 --> 00:05:31,780
For large or larger VR systems or environments, it is quite natural to have a longer duration of VR exposure because the user needs to cover

38
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:34,583
a larger geographical area.

39
00:05:37,647 --> 00:05:47,661
Now, we can reduce the exposure time if we can control the speed of movement, the speed of navigation.

40
00:05:48,623 --> 00:05:58,797
This is quite obvious: the higher the speed is, the lower will be the time to travel and explore the entire geographical area.

41
00:06:04,801 --> 00:06:09,347
So, duration and travel speed are related, intimately related.

42
00:06:10,269 --> 00:06:16,197
Now, duration can be adjusted by adjusting the virtual travel speed, which is quite obvious.

43
00:06:16,698 --> 00:06:22,325
So, higher speed effectively refers to a shorter duration of exposure in the virtual world.

44
00:06:24,829 --> 00:06:31,779
However, to provide a realistic walking experience, we cannot modify the speed arbitrarily.

45
00:06:33,081 --> 00:06:33,441
So,

46
00:06:34,180 --> 00:06:38,185
Teleportation-type movement, of course, would be the best choice.

47
00:06:39,547 --> 00:06:47,757
There, the speed is the maximum, so instantaneous movement occurs between two points, so then the overall duration can be reduced.

48
00:06:47,817 --> 00:06:57,049
However, that may not always be a good way to approach the problem because teleportation is not a natural movement.

49
00:06:57,265 --> 00:06:59,288
Sometimes we look for naturalness.

50
00:06:59,768 --> 00:07:10,323
In that case, such type of movement or any arbitrary speed imposed on the user while navigating in the virtual world may not be a good idea.

51
00:07:16,331 --> 00:07:24,943
There can be other ways to control the duration, such as manipulation of the terrain or the

52
00:07:25,767 --> 00:07:29,371
or the size of the world.

53
00:07:29,391 --> 00:07:41,685
By adjusting the distances between the points of interest in the terrain, by adjusting the terrain of course, we can reduce the travel time and subsequently reduce the exposure time.

54
00:07:44,648 --> 00:07:50,034
But the problem here is that it excludes users from getting an idea about the size of the real space.

55
00:07:50,575 --> 00:07:53,378
So, this is similar to having a teleportation-type movement.

56
00:07:54,235 --> 00:08:01,542
If we arbitrarily change the terrain shape, then the user will not get the true feel of the terrain.

57
00:08:03,824 --> 00:08:19,720
So, that will hamper the user's ability to build a mental map, which in effect can hamper the user's ability to do the wayfinding activities in the virtual world.

58
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:33,852
To get some idea on this mental map, please refer to our earlier lecture on wayfinding, where we talked about the need for a mental map and how it helps in wayfinding.

59
00:08:38,636 --> 00:08:44,443
In the case of a purely system-automated or system-controlled tool, users do not interact.

60
00:08:44,503 --> 00:08:48,087
So, there is no scope for the user to interactively change anything.

61
00:08:49,602 --> 00:08:58,121
So, in that case, we can ignore the interaction time, that is, the time required to interact with the virtual objects.

62
00:09:02,370 --> 00:09:07,341
In such a scenario, when we are talking of an entirely system-controlled VR tool,

63
00:09:10,257 --> 00:09:11,719
the interaction time can be ignored.

64
00:09:11,739 --> 00:09:15,164
However, we are concerned with semi-interactive tours.

65
00:09:15,405 --> 00:09:18,089
That means we are not totally disregarding interactions.

66
00:09:18,669 --> 00:09:24,698
Users can still interact with objects after a navigation task is complete.

67
00:09:24,718 --> 00:09:35,995
So, there the travel will be automatic, system-controlled, but the interactions can happen on a limited scale only at the points of interest,

68
00:09:36,937 --> 00:09:43,967
or a small set of objects, all objects that are present in the virtual environment.

69
00:09:43,987 --> 00:09:48,833
Only with those objects are users allowed to interact in the virtual world.

70
00:09:49,534 --> 00:09:52,198
With other objects, users cannot interact.

71
00:09:52,739 --> 00:10:03,754
So, if such a scenario is assumed, then we can call it a semi-interactive tour: interaction with POIs and navigation by system control.

72
00:10:06,181 --> 00:10:12,851
So, the same idea we have used while formulating the natural walking speed model.

73
00:10:13,332 --> 00:10:24,672
So, you can refer to that part of the lecture, the previous lecture, to get some idea on this assumption: that movement from one point to another is controlled by the system tool, whereas

74
00:10:24,672 --> 00:10:31,800
at specific chosen points called points of interest, users can interact with those.

75
00:10:37,096 --> 00:10:50,609
So, our objective is to deal with semi-interactive system control tools and thereby reduce the duration of exposure to the virtual world.

76
00:10:50,949 --> 00:10:51,630
What can we do?

77
00:10:52,811 --> 00:11:01,639
We can limit the interaction time at the points of interest based on an appropriate time.

78
00:11:01,739 --> 00:11:06,023
So we have to a priori estimate how much time at the most

79
00:11:06,577 --> 00:11:17,595
an average user requires to interact with a point of interest, and accordingly we will allow the user to have that much time at most; after

80
00:11:17,595 --> 00:11:23,777
that, the system will automatically force the user to move to another point.

81
00:11:23,797 --> 00:11:35,250
Now, this idea of a semi-interactive tour and appropriate time of interaction—so there is a limit on the time of interaction—can be utilized

82
00:11:35,872 --> 00:11:42,541
to optimize the duration of exposure in a virtual tour in either of two ways.

83
00:11:44,904 --> 00:12:02,267
One is that we can have an optimum path that lets the users experience all points of interest in minimum time, so the user will be experiencing all points of interest, but

84
00:12:02,872 --> 00:12:10,162
the total time required to navigate plus interact will be minimized.

85
00:12:10,182 --> 00:12:26,565
Second is, given a fixed time—so we have now a time limit for both navigation and interaction—an optimum path to experience the maximum POIs can be found.

86
00:12:26,585 --> 00:12:31,191
So, in the first case, we have an optimum path that allows the user to interact

87
00:12:31,660 --> 00:12:33,602
and explore all points of interest.

88
00:12:34,563 --> 00:12:45,614
In the second case, we have an optimum path that allows the users to interact with the maximum possible number of points of interest within a given fixed time.

89
00:12:47,817 --> 00:13:00,570
So, with either of these two approaches or problems, we can address how to come up with an optimum VR tour where the duration of exposure is optimized.

90
00:13:00,955 --> 00:13:08,172
And by optimizing the duration of exposure, we are expecting that the prevalence of cyber sickness,

91
00:13:08,172 --> 00:13:14,093
Onset of cyber sickness may be optimized because duration and cyber sickness are directly related.

92
00:13:15,995 --> 00:13:27,030
So, let us talk about the first problem, that is, optimum travel path covering all the POIs or points of interest.

93
00:13:29,525 --> 00:13:38,917
So, in order to solve this problem, what we need to do first is to formulate the problem in the form of a graph.

94
00:13:38,937 --> 00:13:43,002
So, we need a graph theoretic formulation of the problem.

95
00:13:43,843 --> 00:13:54,276
So, we can think of the virtual world, that is part of the virtual experience, as a weighted graph.

96
00:13:54,296 --> 00:13:56,038
We can represent it with g.

97
00:13:56,677 --> 00:14:01,022
V and E, V is set of vertices, E is set of edges.

98
00:14:01,182 --> 00:14:06,969
So, V or the vertex set represents all the points of interest.

99
00:14:08,130 --> 00:14:24,810
So, each point of interest we are assuming to be a node or vertex in the graph, and E is the weighted edge set that is the

100
00:14:25,145 --> 00:14:28,229
weighted edges between all the POIs.

101
00:14:29,830 --> 00:14:36,238
Now, each edge weight denotes the Euclidean distance between the pair of vertices.

102
00:14:36,598 --> 00:14:46,714
So, when we say that we are utilizing weights, we are assigning weights to the edges, that is, the path from one POI to another,

103
00:14:46,714 --> 00:14:54,139
the weight we are determining based on the Euclidean distance between these two POIs.

104
00:14:59,232 --> 00:15:06,784
Effectively, G represents a top view of the whole virtual world under consideration.

105
00:15:06,804 --> 00:15:17,822
So, we need to come up with a G, that is the graph which represents the whole virtual world, a kind of top view of the virtual world.

106
00:15:20,386 --> 00:15:22,489
That is the first thing that we need to do.

107
00:15:24,764 --> 00:15:29,209
One important thing here to note is that G need not be complete.

108
00:15:29,510 --> 00:15:40,282
So, while going for this graph theoretic formulation, we need not go for finding a graph that is complete.

109
00:15:42,105 --> 00:15:49,253
Because in a realistic environment, there may not always be a direct path from one place to another.

110
00:15:50,060 --> 00:15:53,725
Now, an example you can see here in this virtual world.

111
00:15:53,745 --> 00:15:58,831
So, on the left-hand side, the colored image is the virtual world having these objects.

112
00:16:01,855 --> 00:16:07,883
Several objects are there, and objects within objects can be points of interest.

113
00:16:07,943 --> 00:16:11,047
So, a single object does not mean a single node.

114
00:16:11,067 --> 00:16:14,131
A single point of interest is a node.

115
00:16:14,151 --> 00:16:19,257
So, for example, the swimming pool here is a single object V4.

116
00:16:20,418 --> 00:16:32,115
Then it shows that there can be multiple objects from this 1, 2, 3, 4.

117
00:16:34,678 --> 00:16:46,375
Then this one can map to this object, this node V3, and each of these can map to these nodes.

118
00:16:49,645 --> 00:17:03,520
So, we can map this structure to this node V7, and V8, V9, V6 are some of the points of interest which may be located in this region.

119
00:17:04,601 --> 00:17:16,935
So, effectively, although we see that there are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 structures, there can be more than that number of

120
00:17:17,354 --> 00:17:24,404
points of interest; each structure may contain multiple points of interest, and accordingly we have to decide on the nodes and the edges.

121
00:17:26,547 --> 00:17:41,848
Now, in this graph theoretic formulation of this virtual world, you can see that this is not a complete graph because there is no edge from, say, V3 to V4 or V4 to V7 and so on.

122
00:17:41,988 --> 00:17:43,851
So, there are many edges,

123
00:17:44,371 --> 00:17:48,938
many pairs of vertices where there is no direct path between them.

124
00:17:48,958 --> 00:17:50,440
And that is the nature of the problem.

125
00:17:50,821 --> 00:17:57,270
In realistic environments, we cannot expect that there will always be a direct path.

126
00:17:58,572 --> 00:18:06,204
So, we can have graphs that are not complete.

127
00:18:06,224 --> 00:18:11,712
Now, for every pair of vertices V1, V2 belonging to this vertex set V,

128
00:18:13,278 --> 00:18:25,871
the edge weights can be defined as the weight epsilon v1, v2 is the Euclidean distance between v1, v2 if

129
00:18:25,871 --> 00:18:33,892
they are directly connected or a very large number denoted by this infinity symbol if they are not directly connected.

130
00:18:35,695 --> 00:18:37,598
So, d is the Euclidean distance.

131
00:18:39,029 --> 00:18:47,720
So, if the two vertices are directly connected, then we can compute the Euclidean distance between them and assign that as the edge weight.

132
00:18:48,001 --> 00:18:58,554
If they are not directly connected, then we can assume that they are having a weight of a very, very, very large number which we are representing with this infinity symbol.

133
00:19:03,681 --> 00:19:05,323
So, given this background,

134
00:19:07,008 --> 00:19:08,971
let us formally state the problem.

135
00:19:10,452 --> 00:19:25,332
Our problem is to find a path P in the given graph G which covers all vertices in V and finishes in the least possible time while maintaining a realistic speed throughout.

136
00:19:27,655 --> 00:19:36,306
So, once we have formulated the problem in the form of a graph, then we can state the problem as that.

137
00:19:37,467 --> 00:19:45,217
We want to find a path within this graph P which includes all the nodes because we are interested in exploring all points of interest.

138
00:19:47,601 --> 00:20:04,183
However, while exploring all points of interest, the objective is to minimize the time required to do so while maintaining a realistic speed so that the user gets a realistic feeling.

139
00:20:08,467 --> 00:20:13,491
So let us now see how we can come up with an algorithm that solves this problem.

140
00:20:13,892 --> 00:20:25,882
So the first stage of the algorithm is to compute the time to travel between two nodes which are directly connected with natural walking speed.

141
00:20:25,922 --> 00:20:28,024
That is the first problem we need to resolve.

142
00:20:30,245 --> 00:20:35,670
Now we can make use of our earlier natural walking model to resolve this problem.

143
00:20:36,751 --> 00:20:38,052
If you recollect,

144
00:20:38,234 --> 00:20:47,849
Earlier we talked about the instantaneous walking speed, which is realistic as represented by V perceived.

145
00:20:47,930 --> 00:20:55,482
So here we are talking of perceived instantaneous walking speed, which can be represented in this form 2.1.

146
00:20:56,604 --> 00:21:02,453
That is the visual gain into the actual instantaneous realistic walking speed V act.

147
00:21:03,310 --> 00:21:10,898
Now, this speed, of course, we know from our basic knowledge of calculus that speed can be represented as dx by dt.

148
00:21:15,943 --> 00:21:26,955
So, then we can say by rearranging the terms that dt, that is this denominator here, is equal to dx by 2.1 into dt.

149
00:21:27,340 --> 00:21:33,611
The act or the actual instantaneous walking speed which is realistic.

150
00:21:36,035 --> 00:21:38,439
So, dt is instantaneous time.

151
00:21:38,940 --> 00:21:50,901
Now, if we want to find out the total time, then we need to take an integral of dt over a distance d.

152
00:21:55,876 --> 00:22:11,252
So this is the integration from 0 to D of dt, or we can say that this is the integration between 0 and D of dx divided by 2.1 times V actual.

153
00:22:12,533 --> 00:22:23,284
That is the formula using which we can compute the total travel time between two nodes.

154
00:22:25,846 --> 00:22:30,915
where the distance between the nodes when they are directly connected is D.

155
00:22:30,915 --> 00:22:46,572
We also know that V actual can be represented in the form f x divided by 0.15 times 157 times h divided by 1.7, all squared.

156
00:22:51,260 --> 00:22:56,205
For reference, you can refer to the previous lecture where we talked about these models and terms.

157
00:22:57,547 --> 00:23:12,884
Now, f is the instantaneous step frequency and h is the height of the user.

158
00:23:12,964 --> 00:23:18,870
So, after simplification, we can get this total time integral

159
00:23:19,373 --> 00:23:26,294
over dt between 0 and d. That is the integral from 0 to d of dx divided by 2.1 times V act, or

160
00:23:26,294 --> 00:23:39,958
the integral from 0 to d of dx divided by 2.1 into this V act formula, that is f x divided by 0.157 times h divided by 1.72, all squared.

161
00:23:42,461 --> 00:23:48,148
Or by rearranging the terms, we can say that dt equals

162
00:23:49,529 --> 00:24:01,023
this term: 0.157 times 1.72 squared divided by 2.1 times h squared, times dx divided by f x squared.

163
00:24:02,905 --> 00:24:13,197
Now, this we can further simplify and rewrite in this form: 1 divided by Vm times dx divided by f x squared.

164
00:24:15,019 --> 00:24:18,844
Now, here Vm is a constant for a specific height.

165
00:24:19,313 --> 00:24:27,742
which is expressed in this way: 2.1 times h squared divided by 0.157 times 1.72 squared.

166
00:24:28,923 --> 00:24:34,286
f(x) we can represent using this expression,

167
00:24:34,286 --> 00:24:40,987
whole expression, 4 by pi into 1.8 into summation over k equal to 0 to d by 2 minus 1,

168
00:24:40,987 --> 00:24:48,004
1 by 2k plus 1 sine of 2k plus 1 omega x. So, it is a variable over x where x is the

169
00:24:49,857 --> 00:24:56,967
instantaneous point.

170
00:24:56,987 --> 00:25:01,934
In fact, this f(x) term we can further simplify using the Taylor expansion.

171
00:25:03,496 --> 00:25:17,535
So, 1 by f(x) square we can simplify as approximately 3 minus 2 square x by 1.8 square, where the square x term is

172
00:25:19,135 --> 00:25:29,798
represented by this one, this expression 4 by pi summation over k, k equal to 0 to d by 2 minus 1,

173
00:25:29,798 --> 00:25:45,240
1 by 2k plus 1 sin of 2k plus 1 omega x. Now, that is the formulation of the time between a pair of nodes which are directly connected.

174
00:25:45,581 --> 00:25:46,982
Now, solving the integral

175
00:25:48,481 --> 00:25:59,194
DT with all these simplifications, rearrangements, and Fourier series expansions up to the term N naught,

176
00:25:59,194 --> 00:26:11,957
we can get travel time estimate tau D between a directly connected vertex pair as tau D equal to D by V naught.

177
00:26:13,895 --> 00:26:20,183
and this term 3 minus 16 by pi square into summation k equal to 1 to n naught of 1 by 2k minus 1 square.

178
00:26:20,564 --> 00:26:33,100
So, this is the result of the computation for DT after using all the simplifications that we have mentioned.

179
00:26:33,120 --> 00:26:37,185
So, as you can see, it is a function of d.

180
00:26:41,283 --> 00:26:49,414
Now, V naught is 2.1 into h square by 0.157 into 1.72 square into 1.8 square.

181
00:26:49,815 --> 00:26:53,140
So, this is a constant for a given height.

182
00:27:02,613 --> 00:27:09,703
So, if we formulate it in this way, then we can say with certainty that the travel time computed

183
00:27:10,493 --> 00:27:16,522
travel time between a pair of directly connected vertices with natural walking speed we can compute.

184
00:27:20,367 --> 00:27:32,311
So, our calculation so far assumes this natural walking model, and accordingly we can claim that the time that we are calculating for traveling from one POI to the next, which are

185
00:27:32,311 --> 00:27:39,235
directly connected, is the travel time when we are assuming a natural walking speed.

186
00:27:40,278 --> 00:27:47,366
Now, this is between pairs of vertices, and we need to estimate the total time for the entire graph.

187
00:27:48,988 --> 00:28:07,450
Now, this problem we can mathematically formulate as total time P equal to i equal to 1 to n minus 1 time between each pair of vertices tau epsilon vi vi plus 1, where i

188
00:28:07,970 --> 00:28:09,993
varies from 1 to n minus 1.

189
00:28:10,494 --> 00:28:22,592
So, we need to find out the times between each pair and then add them up, where P is a path in the graph.

190
00:28:22,673 --> 00:28:35,412
So, we are finding out the total time to travel given a path P, and here

191
00:28:35,881 --> 00:28:43,189
P contains n number of nodes, we are assuming, and then we are calculating the total time to cover the entire path.

192
00:28:45,071 --> 00:28:58,465
And this is the edge length we have already seen before; epsilon vi vi plus 1 is the edge weight or length, the Euclidean distance between the pair of vertices.

193
00:29:00,888 --> 00:29:04,892
So, given all such paths, what we need is to minimize

194
00:29:05,902 --> 00:29:06,843
this travel time.

195
00:29:06,944 --> 00:29:25,172
So, we can recast it as minimize for P given a path where for all V belonging to V and V belonging to P. So, this travel path contains nodes.

