00:29:26,093 --> 00:29:33,765
When we minimize, we need to ensure that all the vertices of the graph are included in P.

197
00:29:37,373 --> 00:29:48,587
That we have to ensure, and then we have to find out a path satisfying these constraints which gives the minimum duration of travel.

198
00:29:52,472 --> 00:29:59,101
Now, this problem is a variation of the classical and celebrated travelling salesman problem, or TSP.

199
00:29:59,641 --> 00:30:01,744
Many of us may be familiar with this problem.

200
00:30:02,973 --> 00:30:18,506
Which says that, given a complete graph—note the term complete graph—what is the shortest possible path that visits each node exactly once and returns to the starting node?

201
00:30:18,526 --> 00:30:27,935
The shortest path is defined based on the total weight of edges in the path.

202
00:30:27,955 --> 00:30:32,018
However, in our case, we did not assume that the graph

203
00:30:32,622 --> 00:30:37,967
that we are creating is a complete graph.

204
00:30:37,987 --> 00:30:43,973
So, there may be cases where there is no Hamiltonian path in G, or in other words, a path where all nodes are

205
00:30:43,973 --> 00:30:52,201
covered and each node is visited exactly once, because we are not assuming it to be a complete graph.

206
00:30:52,221 --> 00:31:00,469
So, one solution to this problem can be to introduce a hypothetical edge between vertices having no edge,

207
00:31:01,630 --> 00:31:03,191
or no direct connection.

208
00:31:03,211 --> 00:31:09,217
So, when there is no direct connection, we can introduce a hypothetical edge for the sake of computation.

209
00:31:10,958 --> 00:31:16,043
And the length of the hypothetical edge is the shortest path length between the vertices.

210
00:31:16,083 --> 00:31:20,436
So, if there are two vertices which are not directly connected,

211
00:31:20,436 --> 00:31:31,637
Then we have to identify the shortest path between these two vertices and the corresponding sum of edge weights for the shortest path, and then that

212
00:31:32,377 --> 00:31:39,268
edge weight sum of the shortest path will be the weight of the hypothetical edge between these two vertices.

213
00:31:43,895 --> 00:31:48,423
Now, the shortest path is well-defined as G is connected.

214
00:31:48,483 --> 00:31:56,836
So, there is no issue in finding the shortest path because G is a connected graph.

215
00:31:56,856 --> 00:32:00,021
So, then what is the modified definition of edge weight?

216
00:32:00,642 --> 00:32:12,618
If we are assuming the presence of hypothetical edges, let us call this modified edge weight definition to be epsilon dash v1 v2, that is the weight between two nodes v1 and v2,

217
00:32:12,618 --> 00:32:17,064
which is d v1 v2 if they are directly connected,

218
00:32:17,064 --> 00:32:27,831
d is the Euclidean distance, or the shortest path v1 v2 if they are not directly connected, that means the sum of the edge weights in the shortest path.

219
00:32:30,832 --> 00:32:35,637
Or, in other words, SP here indicates the shortest path length between the vertex pair.

220
00:32:38,720 --> 00:32:41,684
So, then we need to revise the problem.

221
00:32:43,145 --> 00:32:52,455
So, we have a modified graph G dash with a modified set of edges V and epsilon dash.

222
00:32:52,475 --> 00:32:55,058
Here we are introducing newer edges, hypothetical edges.

223
00:32:55,078 --> 00:32:56,940
So, the edge set changes.

224
00:32:58,118 --> 00:33:08,973
Now, this modified graph is an instance of the TSP problem, and we need to solve for the optimum path.

225
00:33:10,175 --> 00:33:15,943
In order to do that, we can use standard TSP solver approaches or solving algorithms.

226
00:33:16,804 --> 00:33:21,851
We do not need to find a new algorithm, of course, because we are mapping our problem to the standard problem.

227
00:33:24,635 --> 00:33:27,940
Now, with this approach of using a standard solver,

228
00:33:28,679 --> 00:33:29,580
There is one problem.

229
00:33:30,442 --> 00:33:43,901
The output of the TSP solver will give us a path on the graph G' that has an edge set containing hypothetical edges, not on the original graph G where there are no such hypothetical edges.

230
00:33:44,502 --> 00:33:48,247
So, what we need to do is to take care of this problem.

231
00:33:48,928 --> 00:33:58,382
So, the solution obtained using a TSP solver may have some hypothetical edges that are present in G' but not in the original graph G.

232
00:33:59,560 --> 00:34:03,063
Of course, that is the basic idea.

233
00:34:04,024 --> 00:34:09,309
So, we need to replace such edges in the optimal path with the shortest path sp.

234
00:34:13,353 --> 00:34:22,661
So, some more work we have to do on the output provided by a standard TSP solver or solving algorithm.

235
00:34:24,382 --> 00:34:26,985
We have to modify

236
00:34:27,217 --> 00:34:34,886
the output graph output path with replacement of the hypothetical edges in the path with the shortest path.

237
00:34:36,588 --> 00:34:41,773
So, with this basic understanding of the algorithm, let us now see the steps of the algorithm.

238
00:34:42,393 --> 00:34:54,846
Now, here our input is the original graph G with the vertex set V and edge set epsilon, and the output is the optimal time T and path P.

239
00:34:56,699 --> 00:35:06,734
First, we need to compute the complete graph g dash v epsilon dash; that is, we need to compute the hypothetical edge lengths and replace

240
00:35:06,734 --> 00:35:13,221
the edge set with an edge set having hypothetical edges so that we can convert the original graph to a complete graph.

241
00:35:15,164 --> 00:35:22,774
So, for i equal to 1 to n minus 1, do for j equal to 1 to n do, so n is the total number of nodes.

242
00:35:23,935 --> 00:35:30,827
If epsilon v i v j is less than infinity, then epsilon dash v i v j equals.

243
00:35:30,847 --> 00:35:34,212
So, if they are directly connected, then there is no need to change anything.

244
00:35:34,833 --> 00:35:46,112
Otherwise, you change the edge with the shortest path between the vertices.

245
00:35:49,805 --> 00:35:56,294
Next, we need to replace the edge weights or distances in gv dash with travel time t.

246
00:35:56,294 --> 00:36:18,968
For i equal to again 1 to n, for j equal to 1 to n, for i equal to 1 to n minus 1, j equal to 1 to n, the edge weight between this pair of vertices is the time

247
00:36:22,036 --> 00:36:30,047
taken to travel from one vertex to another, which defines the edge.

248
00:36:31,349 --> 00:36:42,553
So, first we have modified the graph to a complete graph; then, in order to come up with a travelling salesman problem, we are modifying the edge weights. Earlier it was the

249
00:36:42,553 --> 00:36:50,254
Euclidean distance; now we are replacing the edge weights with the time to travel between the pair of vertices.

250
00:36:53,423 --> 00:37:02,233
Once that is done, then we have a complete graph where the edge weights are the time to travel between the pair of vertices defining the edge.

251
00:37:04,276 --> 00:37:23,278
Then we are ready to pass it on to a TSP solver, any standard TSP solver; there are lots available where the input will be the modified graph G double dash V epsilon double dash. Now this,

252
00:37:23,562 --> 00:37:36,559
modified graph contains edge sets having edges with weights that are travelling time between the pair of vertices, not Euclidean distance or distance between the pair of vertices.

253
00:37:38,702 --> 00:37:48,855
So, once we pass it on, we get an optimal time and path as output of the TSP solver.

